// games/sonic4/test/poison/poison_two_restores.emp — POISON (see README).
//
// TARGETS: raster_program's one-band-per-program count (spec §4.1).
// EXPECTED FRAGMENT: "one band per program"
//
// TWO WELL-FORMED BANDS, and that is the parcel-P1 change to this fixture. It used to
// be two bare `pal_restore` fires with no ON ops at all — a shape that only ever tripped
// the count because every other band guard was gated behind the count. With ownership
// declared on the op (band-ownership design §2, parcel P1) the guards no longer need
// that gate, so a poison for the count has to be a program the ownership walks ADMIT:
// two textbook bands over DISJOINT CRAM spans, which is one of the two cases the design
// identifies as newly legal and which the walks pass by name.
//
// So the only thing wrong here is the count itself — the P1 refusal that survives, and
// the one parcel P2a deletes. What that count is now about is the RUNTIME, which decides
// emit-or-suppress per patch-table entry with no notion of a pair, and the 64-word
// program buffer, which caps N at three (design §7.1). It is no longer about the guards
// being written in the singular.
//
// COUNT 1: exactly the count. The ownership walks say nothing — which is the half of
// this poison that would go silent if they started refusing legal bands, and the reason
// this file is a better fixture than the one it replaces.
//
// NO IMPORTS, like every ordinary author's module: raster_dsl and engine.vdp are
// COMPTIME_HELPERS members, glob-injected into every module by the real build.
module games.sonic4.test.poison.poison_two_restores

const POISON_TWO_RESTORES = compose([
    band(100, 140, stream_cram($48, [$0EEE, $0AAA, $0666]), 0),
    band(160, 200, stream_cram($68, [$0E00, $0A00]), 0),
])

// THE ELABORATION HOOK. A comptime fn is only elaborated when it is CALLED and an
// unreferenced const is inert, so the guard under test runs only because this
// module-level ensure forces raster_program's body. The predicate is deliberately
// trivially-true: the diagnostic under test comes from INSIDE the call, and this
// ensure's own message is what you would see if the poison ever stopped reaching it.
ensure(raster_program(POISON_TWO_RESTORES).len > 0,
       "poison_two_restores: raster_program returned nothing — this poison no longer reaches the guard it targets")
